(minor spoilers)

I was working on my fourth ascension, and my first with a wizard.
Things were going fairly well so far; aside from some +5 studded
leather early on, a chameleon on dlvl7 who decided to play arch-lich,
and an incident involving the phrase "the sergeant wields a
cockatrice corpse" which I never want to see again, the RNG had been
fairly mild, not doing much for good or ill.  I was a bit short on
gear for most of the early game, but once I found a magic marker I
wrote +poly and +identify and was able to polypile for most of what I
needed. I had a magic lamp, but since I would be able to get the
Eye of A from the quest, I decided to keep it as a light source and
try for wishless conduct.  The polypiling made that fairly easy, and
by the time I reached the castle, the wand was only mildly tempting.

I lost my "LS while nurse dancing; I was blinded and walked over a
cockatrice corpse while not wearing gloves.  I didn't have a spare,
so I did a controlled dive through Gehennom to get the one in Vlad's
tower.  I got the "LS and the Candelabrum, put on the former and
took the latter back to my stash, then mapped the rest of the mazes.
I returned to my stash to re-read some books and get any gear I'd
need for my run before going to wake up Rodney, and as I walked down
the stairs from Sokoban...the game crashed.

Now, this wasn't the first time it had crashed. I was using the
Vulture's Eye tileset, which is a bit unstable at times, so I wasn't
too concerned. But out of paranoia, I backed up the level files
before running recover.  Good thing too, since when I restored my
save...

panic Memory allocation failure; cannot get 620822592 bytes

...Well, that's not good.  I copied the level files back over and
tried recover again, with the same result.  I checked to see if I'd
made any backups of the savefile itself before the crash. I had one,
but recover had always worked before, so I hadn't bothered keeping
it up to date; it was probably 30,000 turns old.  (Note that this
was a backup for genuine backup purposes; I was not savescumming.)
There wouldn't have been much point in loading from it; it'd be
almost as bad as starting over.  So, I could either give up the game,
or try to repair the corrupt savefile.  Well, if there's one thing
that can drive me to obsession more than an ascension run, it's a
good C programming problem.  The former option was right out; I was
going to fix this save.

I grabbed a copy of the source and compiled with debug mode on, then
set a breakpoint in the panic function and examined the call stack
that led to it.  The signs were promising; it looked like it was
the level data that was corrupt, not my character. (I'd written down
some information about what I had when the game crashed, but not
enough to restore my entire character if it proved unrecoverable.)
So knowing that, the question was, what level was corrupt?  I loaded
the old save and copied the level files it put in the playground into
one directory, and put the level files from the corrupt version in
another directory.  I then systematically replaced clusters of level
files from the corrupt version with the same levels from the old
save until the game stopped crashing.  Once it did that, I continued
to narrow it down until I'd isolated the one level file responsible.
The bad news was, the level in question turned out to be the first
level of Sokoban...my primary stash.  And the old save was from well
before I had anything especially good there. But I was a wizard, so
my dependence on wands was lower than usual, and I had most of my
ascension kit in my inventory.  Maybe I'd be able to ascend without
my stash.  Closer inspection revealed that no, I wouldn't.  The old
save wasn't just before I got my ascension kit, it was before I got
the Candelabrum.  As the level with Vlad's tower was from the recent
save, that meant that the Candelabrum didn't currently exist anywhere
in the game.  I'd need to modify the level file manually to return
the game to a winnable state.

I went into restore.c and figured out how objects were loaded in from
the save.  It looked like it was just a raw byte copy; the first few
bytes of each object indicated how many bytes of extra information
existed at the end of the normal object structure, and if the extra
length was -1, it indicated the end of the current object chain.
(Each level and each container had a chain of all its objects.)
I wrote a function to dump out some information about the object in
human readable form after reading each in, and ran the program with
the corrupt save to see what it looked like.
Apparently the game read in about 100 objects, mostly junk, then 17
objects into the box with all my good stuff, it read that the next
object had 329 extra bytes (far more than any real object should
have.)  After that point, it of course wasn't likely to find a -1 at
the point where it should have been, so it just kept reading until
it eventually read that the next object should have a bit over 600
million bytes. It couldn't allocate that much memory, so it crashed.

(52, 13) blessed POT_WATER x9  wt=180
(0, 0) UNKNOWN0 x0  wt=65536corpse=65536, artifact=1, extra bytes=257

That's the dump from the first corrupt object, along with a
noncorrupt one for comparison.  Since the weight and corpse numbers
didn't make any sense either, I decided at this point that it wasn't
just the one number that was corrupt; the whole save after that point
probably was. (I suspect that this resulted when the game followed a
garbage pointer when writing the level files out in the first place.)

So I wasn't going to be able to restore my inventory entirely; I'd
have to just add the items I needed manually.  I modified my
dump_object function to also print out the raw bytes of each object,
and searched the save and level files for the sequences.  Oddly, they
didn't seem to exist anywhere in the files I had.  They must have
been encoded somehow, but my port of nethack didn't seem to be doing
any compression, so I couldn't see where it was being decoded.  But
I realized I didn't have to; instead of modifying the save, I'd
just modify restore.c to replace existing items with the ones I
needed.  Additionally, I needed to make sure that none of the objects
in the old version of my stash were ones I'd used in the time since
that save.  I decided to error on the side of fair play; I couldn't
be certain I hadn't used the consumables, so I'd just leave them in
the box when playing, rather than bother modifying them manually.
The spellbooks were a bit of a different issue; some of them might be
needed, and I knew they were still present at least.  I decided to
increment the number of times I'd read each by 2.  Most would have
been less than that, but it was a good upper bound; any spellbooks
that would have faded on the next read in the recent stash would now
do the same in the old stash.

I assembled a list of items I was positive I'd had.  Most came from
the object dump of the corrupt file, since there was no question I
had those.  Additionally, I added the Candelabrum (of course), a
magic marker (0:30), a potion of unholy water, and 5 scrolls of
genocide. I knew I had the scrolls because polypiling had changed
a stack of 12 junk scrolls into genocide, and I had used no more than
5 of them reverse genociding silver dragons and black unicorns.
(I overestimated that to 7, just to be *absolutely* sure I wasn't
giving myself anything I hadn't already had.)  The unholy water would
have been there since I'd cursed the first stack of genocide scroll
and I never use up my last potion of holy or unholy water without
dipping to make more first.  The magic marker was the only thing I
wasn't sure about.  My notes from right after the crash indicated
that I had two markers, both "reasonably well charged", one of which
had been recharged once.  So worst case scenario, at least one of
them would have been able to write a scroll of charging, and the
other a useful scroll like ?EA.  (0:30) was the best guess I could
make.  I won't go into the details of how I modified dump_object to
add the items; it's not particularly interesting and I've rambled on
enough already. It turns out that all the thought I put into this
wasn't especially necessary, since I never used the marker.  I used
two of the scrolls to reverse genocide black unicorns and polypiled
another marker.  I then wrote a ?RC and ?charging, and never used
either. And the only old spellbook I tried to use was faded. So while
most of that effort was wasted, at least I can be positive I didn't
cheat in any way.

I loaded the file with the spliced in old level, checked that the
items were added as they should have been, engraved "Old Stash" under
the box of items that might have been consumed (after taking out a
few nonconsumables) so I wouldn't accidentally use something from it,
then saved the game.  I then loaded it under an unmodified version
of nethack and started playing. After making a backup of the save,
of course.

The ascension run itself was pretty straightforward.  The only real
hangup happened when an ogre king stole magicbane with a bullwhip
in the Sanctum and I didn't notice until I'd climbed up 10 stairs.
But being able to cast finger of death made me a lot less concerned
about Rodney showing up, so I took my time and went back for it.
I was missing some of the gear I'd originally had, of course.  I
would have liked having =conflict on Air, and my pet purple worm
would have been handy on Astral, but both were lost in the crash and
I never restored them.  The Amulet kept draining my magic points, but
my AC was low enough that I was able to use weaker monsters as a
shield against the stronger ones while I fought my way through with
magicbane.  The neutral alter was the second one I tried.

